v so this is a developer mistake, not steady state. |\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGTERM/SIGINT) | Tear down children; do not leave orphaned redis/node processes. |\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGKILL) | Best-effort only; orphans possible — acceptable for grader stop. |\n| Duplicate start of `start.sh` | Second instance should fail on bind; do not require a pidfile unless implementer wants one under `/app/data`. |\n| Health called during Redis outage | Still `200 {\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":N}`. |\n| Extra JSON fields on health | Avoid; stick to exact two fields so graders using exact match do not flake. |\n| `status` value other than `\"ok\"` | Not used on success path. |\n| Pip/venv install failure | Exit non-zero before binding ports; log the pip error. |\n| Working directory | `start.sh` `cd`s to its own directory (`APP_ROOT`) so paths work regardless of caller cwd. |\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. `test -x /app/start.sh` (or `./start.sh` in repo root) succeeds.\n2. After starting `./start.sh` (under `marathon-portlock` when ports might be contested) and waiting for readiness:\n   - `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health` → HTTP 200, JSON `status==\"ok\"`, `node_id==0`\n   - same for `:8001` → `node_id==1`\n   - same for `:8002` → `node_id==2`\n3. `redis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING` → `PONG`.\n4. `start.sh` remains in the foreground (does not daemonize itself and return immediately).\n5. `node_id` values are JSON numbers; response contains no requirement for other keys.\n6. Killing one node’s PID (SIGKILL) does **not** take down the other two health endpoints; within 60s the killed port serves health again with the **same** `node_id`.\n7. Killing `redis-server` does **not** cause health endpoints to fail; Redis accepts `PING` again after `start.sh` respawns it.\n8. No Docker socket usage; no calls to external network services required for health.\n9. A clear module path exists for adding routes (e.g. empty aggregation point in `huddle/api/__init__.py`) so MARATHON-3 can attach `/api/auth/*` without changing `start.sh`.\n10. Negative: binding must **not** use `0.0.0.0` for the three HTTP nodes (must be `127.0.0.1`).\n11. Negative: `/api/health` must **not** return `5xx` solely because Redis is down.\n12. Negative: `node_id` for port 8000 must **not** be `1` or `2` under any restart ordering.\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n**Automated / implementer**\n- Unit: config mapping port↔`node_id`; reject mismatched env.\n- Unit: health handler returns exact payload for each injected `node_id`.\n- Integration (script or pytest): boot via `start.sh` (or invoke three modules + redis in a test harness), assert three health payloads + Redis PING.\n- Chaos smoke: SIGKILL one uvicorn PID and Redis PID; assert criteria 6–7.\n- Always free ports first: `marathon-freeports`; wrap long-lived servers with `marathon-portlock`. Kill all started processes before finishing.\n\n**Human / QA verification**\n```bash\nmarathon-freeports\nmarathon-portlock /app/start.sh   # or ./start.sh from repo root\n# in another shell:\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/api/health\nredis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING\n# SIGKILL one node PID; re-curl within 60s\n# SIGKILL redis; confirm health still 200; wait for PING\n```\n\n**Out of test scope for this task:** auth flows, SPA, IRC, WebSocket seq denseness, Redis fan-out fallback semantics beyond process respawn.\n","localExecutionTimeMs":121},"isBackground":false},"description":"Extract restart-related MARATHON-2 design"},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-58e7a90d-024f-41e5-9308-8ce97b66c80a-12\nfc_f66188ae-518a-9dd1-b878-41719bb8bbae_0","startedAtMs":"1787424721276","completedAtMs":"1787424721561"},"model_call_id":"032aaf07-7b4c-4362-ae7a-9f6554c9a893-4-rydo","session_id":"9ebbcf65-7775-47eb-8335-8c079ff42d53","timestamp_ms":1787424721598}
