ll_id":"a239700f-d856-4f40-b0ea-707f1bd6481c-3-7z8z","session_id":"08c39eea-4edb-41e1-83bc-3c69b9e09ad6","timestamp_ms":1787424883253}
{"type":"tool_call","subtype":"completed","call_id":"call-797cdd47-f954-47c6-9f86-1c5f44fb39d5-8\nfc_93232823-f951-9f66-acd3-b4b9a27aed41_0","tool_call":{"shellToolCall":{"args":{"command":"loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(\"NOTES:\"); print(d.get(\"notes\") or \"(none)\"); print(\"\\n==== DESIGN ====\\n\"); print(d.get(\"design\") or \"(none)\")'","workingDirectory":"","timeout":30000,"toolCallId":"call-797cdd47-f954-47c6-9f86-1c5f44fb39d5-8\nfc_93232823-f951-9f66-acd3-b4b9a27aed41_0","simpleCommands":["loom","python3"],"hasInputRedirect":false,"hasOutputRedirect":false,"parsingResult":{"parsingFailed":false,"executableCommands":[{"name":"loom","args":[{"type":"word","value":"data"},{"type":"word","value":"show"},{"type":"word","value":"MARATHON-2"},{"type":"word","value":"--output"},{"type":"word","value":"json"}],"fullText":"loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json"},{"name":"python3","args":[{"type":"word","value":"-c"},{"type":"raw_string","value":"'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(\"NOTES:\"); print(d.get(\"notes\") or \"(none)\"); print(\"\\n==== DESIGN ====\\n\"); print(d.get(\"design\") or \"(none)\")'"}],"fullText":"python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(\"NOTES:\"); print(d.get(\"notes\") or \"(none)\"); print(\"\\n==== DESIGN ====\\n\"); print(d.get(\"design\") or \"(none)\")'"}],"hasRedirects":false,"hasCommandSubstitution":false,"redirects":[]},"fileOutputThresholdBytes":"40000","isBackground":false,"skipApproval":false,"timeoutBehavior":"TIMEOUT_BEHAVIOR_BACKGROUND","hardTimeout":86400000,"description":"Fetch MARATHON-2 design and notes","closeStdin":true,"conversationId":"08c39eea-4edb-41e1-83bc-3c69b9e09ad6","adminCommandDenylist":[]},"result":{"success":{"command":"loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(\"NOTES:\"); print(d.get(\"notes\") or \"(none)\"); print(\"\\n==== DESIGN ====\\n\"); print(d.get(\"design\") or \"(none)\")'","workingDirectory":"","exitCode":0,"signal":"","stdout":"NOTES:\nImplemented cluster scaffold per design: start.sh supervises Redis+3 FastAPI/Uvicorn nodes; GET /api/health returns {status,node_id}; vendor wheels for offline pip; verified health on :8000/1/2, Redis PING, SIGKILL node+redis restart within ~2s.\n\n==== DESIGN ====\n\n## Summary\n\nDeliver a bootable Huddle cluster skeleton at `/app`: a foreground `/app/start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, plus `GET /api/health` returning `200 {\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":<0|1|2>}` with port→id mapping `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`. This is the foundation every later API/realtime/SPA task mounts onto. No remote services and no Docker-in-Docker. Auth, workspaces, SPA, IRC, WebSocket fan-out, and durable schemas are out of scope here; this task only creates process supervision, shared config conventions, and a stub API router that subsequent tasks extend.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n**Decision:** One Python 3.12 process image, three OS processes, FastAPI + Uvicorn, supervised by bash `start.sh`, with local `redis-server` as a fourth supervised child.\n\n**Why this shape**\n- The product requires three distinct HTTP listeners and node-kill resilience; three OS processes (not one multi-bind process, not threads-only) match the grader’s SIGKILL model and the 60s auto-restart requirement.\n- FastAPI gives a single mountable `APIRouter` tree under `/api` so MARATHON-3+ can add modules without rewriting the HTTP entrypoint.\n- Redis is started now even though health does not use it, because later fan-out and the Redis-outage tests assume the daemon is owned by `start.sh`.\n- Language/framework are unspecified by the product instruction; Python 3.12 is present in the harness and fits REST + later WebSocket work.\n\n**Alternatives considered**\n1. **Node.js `http` / Express** — viable (Node is installed), but the rest of the epic’s backend tasks are naturally one shared Python package with SQLite; splitting runtimes would complicate shared state.\n2. **Python stdlib only** — avoids deps, but forces every later task to reinvent routing, JSON validation, multipart, and WebSockets; rejected for velocity and consistency.\n3. **One process binding all three ports** — fails the “kill one HTTP node” resilience model; rejected.\n4. **Docker Compose / containers** — prohibited (no DinD / no remote orchestration).\n\n**Trade-off accepted:** `start.sh` must ensure FastAPI/Uvicorn (and `redis` client later) are available via a local venv + pinned `requirements.txt`, preferably with wheels under `vendor/` so boot does not depend on live PyPI. Cold start is slightly heavier than stdlib, but the router seam for sibling tasks is far cleaner.\n\n**Conventions this design locks for the epic (no sibling designs exist yet)**\n- Product name: **Huddle**; repo root is `/app` at runtime.\n- HTTP bind: `127.0.0.1` only; ports `8000/8001/8002`.\n- `node_id` ∈ `{0,1,2}` derived solely from port (never from startup order).\n- API prefix: `/api/...`; JSON only for API responses.\n- Primary durable store (for later tasks): SQLite file at `/app/data/huddle.db` shared by all nodes via the local filesystem. Redis is **not** the source of truth for users/messages — it is for pub/sub fan-out (and optional caches) in later tasks.\n- Env contract for each node process:\n  - `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` — integer `0|1|2`\n  - `HUDDLE_PORT` — integer `8000|8001|8002`\n  - `HUDDLE_HOST` — `127.0.0.1`\n  - `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL` — `redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0`\n  - `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR` — `/app/data` (or `$APP_ROOT/data`)\n- Error bodies for unspecified errors: FastAPI default `{\"detail\": ...}` until a later design overrides.\n- Health **must not** check Redis or SQLite liveness; a live HTTP process alone is enough for `200`.\n\n## Interface Contracts\n\n### Process / ops seam (verifier ↔ `start.sh`)\n\n| Concern | Contract |\n|---|---|\n| Entrypoint | Verifier runs `/app/start.sh` (executable). |\n| Lifetime | `start.sh` stays in the **foreground** until killed; while it is alive the cluster serves. |\n| Children | Exactly three HTTP node processes + one `redis-server` (plus any short-lived helpers). |\n| Ports | HTTP: `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, `:8002`. Redis: `127.0.0.1:6379`. |\n| Restart | If an HTTP child exits for any reason (including SIGKILL), `start.sh` respawns that node with the same `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT` so it is healthy again **within 60s** (target: a few seconds). If Redis exits, `start.sh` respawns it promptly so pub/sub can resume after later tasks exist. |\n| Shutdown | On SIGINT/SIGTERM to `start.sh`, terminate children and exit non-zero only on fatal setup failure. |\n| Isolation | No remote URLs, no Docker, no external managed Redis. |\n\n### HTTP seam — `GET /api/health`\n\n**Ownership:** Backend only for this task. No frontend consumer yet; when the SPA exists it may probe health but must not block login on it.\n\n**Request**\n- Method/path: `GET /api/health`\n- Auth: none\n- Query/body: none\n\n**Success response**\n- Status: `200`\n- Headers: `Content-Type: application/json`\n- Body (exact keys; `node_id` is JSON number, not string):\n\n```json\n{\"status\": \"ok\", \"node_id\": 0}\n```\n\n| Port | `node_id` |\n|------|-----------|\n| 8000 | `0` |\n| 8001 | `1` |\n| 8002 | `2` |\n\n**Validation**\n- Server: derive `node_id` from `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` (set by `start.sh` to match the port table). Reject process start if env `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT` disagree with the table.\n- Client (future SPA / grader): treat non-200 or missing keys as unhealthy; while slow, show no hard dependency in UI for this task.\n\n**Failure / slow behavior**\n- If the node process is down: TCP connection refused / timeout — client retries; `start.sh` is responsible for respawn.\n- Health handler itself never waits on Redis/SQLite/network; latency should be trivial (ms).\n- Unknown methods on `/api/health` (e.g. `POST`): `405` or FastAPI default — not required by grader; implementer may leave framework default.\n\n### Stub routing seam (this task ↔ later API tasks)\n\n- Mount a top-level FastAPI app with `app.include_router(..., prefix=\"/api\")`.\n- This task registers only the health router.\n- Later tasks add sibling modules (e.g. `huddle/api/auth.py`) and `include_router` them from `huddle/api/__init__.py` — **no** second HTTP server.\n- Unregistered `/api/*` paths return `404 {\"detail\": ...}` (framework default).\n- `GET /` is **not** implemented here (SPA is a later task). Returning framework `404` is acceptable until the frontend task adds static hosting.\n\n### Cross-node identity seam\n\n- All three nodes run the **same code** and same DB file path; only `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` / `HUDDLE_PORT` differ.\n- Resource ids created in later tasks must be stable cluster-wide (SQLite shared file supports this). This task does not mint resource ids.\n\n## Data Model\n\nNo application schema in this task.\n\n| Store | Role now | Later |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/app/data/` directory | Created/owned by `start.sh` or first boot | SQLite `huddle.db`, uploads, redis working dir |\n| Redis @ `127.0.0.1:6379` | Process up; unused by health | Pub/sub fan-out; must survive kill+respawn |\n| In-process | `node_id`, port from env | Connection pools, WS subscribers |\n\n**Schema changes:** none. Backward compatible by vacuity (empty repo → first schema arrives in MARATHON-3+).\n\n**Redis config expectations (no ACL/password):**\n- `bind 127.0.0.1`\n- `port 6379`\n- `dir` under `/app/data/redis` (or equivalent local path)\n- protected-mode as needed for local-only bind\n- not daemonized by Redis itself — `start.sh` is the supervisor (`--daemonize no`)\n\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\n\n### Create\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `start.sh` | Executable foreground supervisor: deps/venv, Redis, three nodes, restart loop, signal handling. |\n| `requirements.txt` | Pin `fastapi`, `uvicorn[standard]` (and leave room for `redis`, etc. later or add `redis` now unused). Prefer exact pins. |\n| `vendor/` (optional but recommended) | Bundled wheels so `pip install --no-index` works offline. |\n| `huddle/__init__.py` | Package marker; version string optional. |\n| `huddle/config.py` | Read/validate `HUDDLE_*` env; expose `settings.node_id`, `settings.port`, `settings.host`, `settings.redis_url`, `settings.data_dir`. |\n| `huddle/app.py` | `create_app()` → FastAPI instance; include API router; no business routes beyond what’s included. |\n| `huddle/api/__init__.py` | Aggregate `api_router`; include health; comment/marker for sibling routers. |\n| `huddle/api/health.py` | `GET /health` → `{status, node_id}` (mounted under `/api`). |\n| `huddle/__main__.py` | `uvicorn` entry: `python -m huddle` reads settings and serves `huddle.app:create_app`. |\n\n### Modify\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `.gitignore` | Ignore `.venv/`, `__pycache__/`, `data/*` runtime artifacts (keep `data/.gitkeep` if added), `.timer_start`; do **not** ignore `start.sh` or `huddle/`. |\n| (optional) `data/.gitkeep` | Ensure `/app/data` exists in the tree. |\n\n### Do not modify\n\n| File | Reason |\n|---|---|\n| `timer.sh` | Harness timer; unrelated. |\n| `loom-prompts/` | Agent prompts; not product. |\n\n## Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\n\n**This task is the root dependency.** MARATHON-3 (auth), MARATHON-4 (profiles), MARATHON-5 (workspaces), and all later backend tasks `depends_on` this scaffold.\n\n**Build order inside this task**\n1. Package layout + `config` + `health` + `create_app`\n2. `requirements.txt` (+ vendor if used)\n3. `start.sh` supervision last (needs a runnable module)\n\n**Parallelism**\n- Nothing else in the epic can meaningfully implement against live ports until this lands.\n- Design work for MARATHON-3+ can proceed in parallel against the contracts below.\n\n**Seams owned here that dependents rely on**\n\n| Seam | Owner | Consumers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `start.sh` lifecycle + port map + restart | MARATHON-2 | All runtime tasks, QA, grader |\n| `GET /api/health` shape | MARATHON-2 | Grader; ops checks |\n| `huddle.api` router aggregation | MARATHON-2 | MARATHON-3+ route modules |\n| `HUDDLE_*` env + `/app/data` + Redis URL | MARATHON-2 | Auth persistence, files, WS fan-out |\n| SQLite path convention `/app/data/huddle.db` | MARATHON-2 (convention only; no schema) | MARATHON-3+ |\n\n**Explicitly not split:** do not defer HTTP auto-restart to a later “resilience” task — the product grades restart via `start.sh`, and rewriting supervision later is wasteful. Full Redis-outage **fan-out fallback** logic stays in a future realtime task; this task only respawns Redis.\n\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\n\nNeighborhood scan of the repo found only `timer.sh`, empty `data/`, and agent prompt/gitignore files — no existing HTTP/Redis/SPA code. From the product instruction / epic, the following are **intentionally excluded** from MARATHON-2:\n\n| Item | Why excluded |\n|---|---|\n| `GET /` SPA / static assets / `data-testid` hooks | Frontend tasks; not in this task description |\n| Auth register/login/me (MARATHON-3) | Separate task; needs DB schema |\n| Profiles, workspaces, channels, messages, files, search, etc. | Later epic children |\n| WebSocket `/api/ws` + dense `seq` + cross-node fan-out | Realtime task(s); needs Redis pub/sub usage + fallback |\n| IRC gateway `:6667` | Separate protocol task |\n| Redis-outage application-level fan-out fallback (direct peer push) | Requires message pipeline; only process respawn is in scope here |\n| Dependency on Slack SDK / remote APIs | Prohibited entirely |\n\nIf the neighborhood later gains half-built servers on these ports, implementers must replace/integrate with this layout rather than run a second cluster.\n\n## Edge Cases & Error Handling\n\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\n|---|---|\n| Port 8000/8001/8002 already bound at start | Fail fast with a clear stderr message; exit non-zero. Do not silently bind another port. (QA uses `marathon-freeports` / `marathon-portlock`.) |\n| Port 6379 already bound by an alien process | Fail fast if it is not our Redis; if a previous Huddle Redis is healthy, either adopt it or kill-and-replace — **prefer**: if `PING` works on `127.0.0.1:6379`, reuse; otherwise abort with error. |\n| `redis-server` binary missing | Exit non-zero with message; do not pretend health is enough. |\n| HTTP child SIGKILL’d | Supervisor restarts that node only; other nodes keep serving without interruption. |\n| Redis SIGKILL’d | Supervisor restarts Redis; HTTP nodes keep answering `/api/health` (and later writes once those exist). Do **not** crash nodes when Redis drops. |\n| `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` missing/invalid | Node process exits immediately with error; supervisor may restart — ensure `start.sh` always exports valid env so this is a developer mistake, not steady state. |\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGTERM/SIGINT) | Tear down children; do not leave orphaned redis/node processes. |\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGKILL) | Best-effort only; orphans possible — acceptable for grader stop. |\n| Duplicate start of `start.sh` | Second instance should fail on bind; do not require a pidfile unless implementer wants one under `/app/data`. |\n| Health called during Redis outage | Still `200 {\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":N}`. |\n| Extra JSON fields on health | Avoid; stick to exact two fields so graders using exact match do not flake. |\n| `status` value other than `\"ok\"` | Not used on success path. |\n| Pip/venv install failure | Exit non-zero before binding ports; log the pip error. |\n| Working directory | `start.sh` `cd`s to its own directory (`APP_ROOT`) so paths work regardless of caller cwd. |\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. `test -x /app/start.sh` (or `./start.sh` in repo root) succeeds.\n2. After starting `./start.sh` (under `marathon-portlock` when ports might be contested) and waiting for readiness:\n   - `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health` → HTTP 200, JSON `status==\"ok\"`, `node_id==0`\n   - same for `:8001` → `node_id==1`\n   - same for `:8002` → `node_id==2`\n3. `redis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING` → `PONG`.\n4. `start.sh` remains in the foreground (does not daemonize itself and return immediately).\n5. `node_id` values are JSON numbers; response contains no requirement for other keys.\n6. Killing one node’s PID (SIGKILL) does **not** take down the other two health endpoints; within 60s the killed port serves health again with the **same** `node_id`.\n7. Killing `redis-server` does **not** cause health endpoints to fail; Redis accepts `PING` again after `start.sh` respawns it.\n8. No Docker socket usage; no calls to external network services required for health.\n9. A clear module path exists for adding routes (e.g. empty aggregation point in `huddle/api/__init__.py`) so MARATHON-3 can attach `/api/auth/*` without changing `start.sh`.\n10. Negative: binding must **not** use `0.0.0.0` for the three HTTP nodes (must be `127.0.0.1`).\n11. Negative: `/api/health` must **not** return `5xx` solely because Redis is down.\n12. Negative: `node_id` for port 8000 must **not** be `1` or `2` under any restart ordering.\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n**Automated / implementer**\n- Unit: config mapping port↔`node_id`; reject mismatched env.\n- Unit: health handler returns exact payload for each injected `node_id`.\n- Integration (script or pytest): boot via `start.sh` (or invoke three modules + redis in a test harness), assert three health payloads + Redis PING.\n- Chaos smoke: SIGKILL one uvicorn PID and Redis PID; assert criteria 6–7.\n- Always free ports first: `marathon-freeports`; wrap long-lived servers with `marathon-portlock`. Kill all started processes before finishing.\n\n**Human / QA verification**\n```bash\nmarathon-freeports\nmarathon-portlock /app/start.sh   # or ./start.sh from repo root\n# in another shell:\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/api/health\nredis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING\n# SIGKILL one node PID; re-curl within 60s\n# SIGKILL redis; confirm health still 200; wait for PING\n```\n\n**Out of test scope for this task:** auth flows, SPA, IRC, WebSocket seq denseness, Redis fan-out fallback semantics beyond process respawn.\n","stderr":"time=2026-08-22T18:54:43.329Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\n","executionTime":142,"interleavedOutput":"time=2026-08-22T18:54:43.329Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\nNOTES:\nImplemented cluster scaffold per design: start.sh supervises Redis+3 FastAPI/Uvicorn nodes; GET /api/health returns {status,node_id}; vendor wheels for offline pip; verified health on :8000/1/2, Redis PING, SIGKILL node+redis restart within ~2s.\n\n==== DESIGN ====\n\n## Summary\n\nDeliver a bootable Huddle cluster skeleton at `/app`: a foreground `/app/start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, plus `GET /api/health` returning `200 {\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":<0|1|2>}` with port→id mapping `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`. This is the foundation every later API/realtime/SPA task mounts onto. No remote services and no Docker-in-Docker. Auth, workspaces, SPA, IRC, WebSocket fan-out, and durable schemas are out of scope here; this task only creates process supervision, shared config conventions, and a stub API router that subsequent tasks extend.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n**Decision:** One Python 3.12 process image, three OS processes, FastAPI + Uvicorn, supervised by bash `start.sh`, with local `redis-server` as a fourth supervised child.\n\n**Why this shape**\n- The product requires three distinct HTTP listeners and node-kill resilience; three OS processes (not one multi-bind process, not threads-only) match the grader’s SIGKILL model and the 60s auto-restart requirement.\n- FastAPI gives a single mountable `APIRouter` tree under `/api` so MARATHON-3+ can add modules without rewriting the HTTP entrypoint.\n- Redis is started now even though health does not use it, because later fan-out and the Redis-outage tests assume the daemon is owned by `start.sh`.\n- Language/framework are unspecified by the product instruction; Python 3.12 is present in the harness and fits REST + later WebSocket work.\n\n**Alternatives considered**\n1. **Node.js `http` / Express** — viable (Node is installed), but the rest of the epic’s backend tasks are naturally one shared Python package with SQLite; splitting runtimes would complicate shared state.\n2. **Python stdlib only** — avoids deps, but forces every later task to reinvent routing, JSON validation, multipart, and WebSockets; rejected for velocity and consistency.\n3. **One process binding all three ports** — fails the “kill one HTTP node” resilience model; rejected.\n4. **Docker Compose / containers** — prohibited (no DinD / no remote orchestration).\n\n**Trade-off accepted:** `start.sh` must ensure FastAPI/Uvicorn (and `redis` client later) are available via a local venv + pinned `requirements.txt`, preferably with wheels under `vendor/` so boot does not depend on live PyPI. Cold start is slightly heavier than stdlib, but the router seam for sibling tasks is far cleaner.\n\n**Conventions this design locks for the epic (no sibling designs exist yet)**\n- Product name: **Huddle**; repo root is `/app` at runtime.\n- HTTP bind: `127.0.0.1` only; ports `8000/8001/8002`.\n- `node_id` ∈ `{0,1,2}` derived solely from port (never from startup order).\n- API prefix: `/api/...`; JSON only for API responses.\n- Primary durable store (for later tasks): SQLite file at `/app/data/huddle.db` shared by all nodes via the local filesystem. Redis is **not** the source of truth for users/messages — it is for pub/sub fan-out (and optional caches) in later tasks.\n- Env contract for each node process:\n  - `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` — integer `0|1|2`\n  - `HUDDLE_PORT` — integer `8000|8001|8002`\n  - `HUDDLE_HOST` — `127.0.0.1`\n  - `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL` — `redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0`\n  - `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR` — `/app/data` (or `$APP_ROOT/data`)\n- Error bodies for unspecified errors: FastAPI default `{\"detail\": ...}` until a later design overrides.\n- Health **must not** check Redis or SQLite liveness; a live HTTP process alone is enough for `200`.\n\n## Interface Contracts\n\n### Process / ops seam (verifier ↔ `start.sh`)\n\n| Concern | Contract |\n|---|---|\n| Entrypoint | Verifier runs `/app/start.sh` (executable). |\n| Lifetime | `start.sh` stays in the **foreground** until killed; while it is alive the cluster serves. |\n| Children | Exactly three HTTP node processes + one `redis-server` (plus any short-lived helpers). |\n| Ports | HTTP: `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, `:8002`. Redis: `127.0.0.1:6379`. |\n| Restart | If an HTTP child exits for any reason (including SIGKILL), `start.sh` respawns that node with the same `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT` so it is healthy again **within 60s** (target: a few seconds). If Redis exits, `start.sh` respawns it promptly so pub/sub can resume after later tasks exist. |\n| Shutdown | On SIGINT/SIGTERM to `start.sh`, terminate children and exit non-zero only on fatal setup failure. |\n| Isolation | No remote URLs, no Docker, no external managed Redis. |\n\n### HTTP seam — `GET /api/health`\n\n**Ownership:** Backend only for this task. No frontend consumer yet; when the SPA exists it may probe health but must not block login on it.\n\n**Request**\n- Method/path: `GET /api/health`\n- Auth: none\n- Query/body: none\n\n**Success response**\n- Status: `200`\n- Headers: `Content-Type: application/json`\n- Body (exact keys; `node_id` is JSON number, not string):\n\n```json\n{\"status\": \"ok\", \"node_id\": 0}\n```\n\n| Port | `node_id` |\n|------|-----------|\n| 8000 | `0` |\n| 8001 | `1` |\n| 8002 | `2` |\n\n**Validation**\n- Server: derive `node_id` from `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` (set by `start.sh` to match the port table). Reject process start if env `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT` disagree with the table.\n- Client (future SPA / grader): treat non-200 or missing keys as unhealthy; while slow, show no hard dependency in UI for this task.\n\n**Failure / slow behavior**\n- If the node process is down: TCP connection refused / timeout — client retries; `start.sh` is responsible for respawn.\n- Health handler itself never waits on Redis/SQLite/network; latency should be trivial (ms).\n- Unknown methods on `/api/health` (e.g. `POST`): `405` or FastAPI default — not required by grader; implementer may leave framework default.\n\n### Stub routing seam (this task ↔ later API tasks)\n\n- Mount a top-level FastAPI app with `app.include_router(..., prefix=\"/api\")`.\n- This task registers only the health router.\n- Later tasks add sibling modules (e.g. `huddle/api/auth.py`) and `include_router` them from `huddle/api/__init__.py` — **no** second HTTP server.\n- Unregistered `/api/*` paths return `404 {\"detail\": ...}` (framework default).\n- `GET /` is **not** implemented here (SPA is a later task). Returning framework `404` is acceptable until the frontend task adds static hosting.\n\n### Cross-node identity seam\n\n- All three nodes run the **same code** and same DB file path; only `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` / `HUDDLE_PORT` differ.\n- Resource ids created in later tasks must be stable cluster-wide (SQLite shared file supports this). This task does not mint resource ids.\n\n## Data Model\n\nNo application schema in this task.\n\n| Store | Role now | Later |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/app/data/` directory | Created/owned by `start.sh` or first boot | SQLite `huddle.db`, uploads, redis working dir |\n| Redis @ `127.0.0.1:6379` | Process up; unused by health | Pub/sub fan-out; must survive kill+respawn |\n| In-process | `node_id`, port from env | Connection pools, WS subscribers |\n\n**Schema changes:** none. Backward compatible by vacuity (empty repo → first schema arrives in MARATHON-3+).\n\n**Redis config expectations (no ACL/password):**\n- `bind 127.0.0.1`\n- `port 6379`\n- `dir` under `/app/data/redis` (or equivalent local path)\n- protected-mode as needed for local-only bind\n- not daemonized by Redis itself — `start.sh` is the supervisor (`--daemonize no`)\n\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\n\n### Create\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `start.sh` | Executable foreground supervisor: deps/venv, Redis, three nodes, restart loop, signal handling. |\n| `requirements.txt` | Pin `fastapi`, `uvicorn[standard]` (and leave room for `redis`, etc. later or add `redis` now unused). Prefer exact pins. |\n| `vendor/` (optional but recommended) | Bundled wheels so `pip install --no-index` works offline. |\n| `huddle/__init__.py` | Package marker; version string optional. |\n| `huddle/config.py` | Read/validate `HUDDLE_*` env; expose `settings.node_id`, `settings.port`, `settings.host`, `settings.redis_url`, `settings.data_dir`. |\n| `huddle/app.py` | `create_app()` → FastAPI instance; include API router; no business routes beyond what’s included. |\n| `huddle/api/__init__.py` | Aggregate `api_router`; include health; comment/marker for sibling routers. |\n| `huddle/api/health.py` | `GET /health` → `{status, node_id}` (mounted under `/api`). |\n| `huddle/__main__.py` | `uvicorn` entry: `python -m huddle` reads settings and serves `huddle.app:create_app`. |\n\n### Modify\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `.gitignore` | Ignore `.venv/`, `__pycache__/`, `data/*` runtime artifacts (keep `data/.gitkeep` if added), `.timer_start`; do **not** ignore `start.sh` or `huddle/`. |\n| (optional) `data/.gitkeep` | Ensure `/app/data` exists in the tree. |\n\n### Do not modify\n\n| File | Reason |\n|---|---|\n| `timer.sh` | Harness timer; unrelated. |\n| `loom-prompts/` | Agent prompts; not product. |\n\n## Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\n\n**This task is the root dependency.** MARATHON-3 (auth), MARATHON-4 (profiles), MARATHON-5 (workspaces), and all later backend tasks `depends_on` this scaffold.\n\n**Build order inside this task**\n1. Package layout + `config` + `health` + `create_app`\n2. `requirements.txt` (+ vendor if used)\n3. `start.sh` supervision last (needs a runnable module)\n\n**Parallelism**\n- Nothing else in the epic can meaningfully implement against live ports until this lands.\n- Design work for MARATHON-3+ can proceed in parallel against the contracts below.\n\n**Seams owned here that dependents rely on**\n\n| Seam | Owner | Consumers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `start.sh` lifecycle + port map + restart | MARATHON-2 | All runtime tasks, QA, grader |\n| `GET /api/health` shape | MARATHON-2 | Grader; ops checks |\n| `huddle.api` router aggregation | MARATHON-2 | MARATHON-3+ route modules |\n| `HUDDLE_*` env + `/app/data` + Redis URL | MARATHON-2 | Auth persistence, files, WS fan-out |\n| SQLite path convention `/app/data/huddle.db` | MARATHON-2 (convention only; no schema) | MARATHON-3+ |\n\n**Explicitly not split:** do not defer HTTP auto-restart to a later “resilience” task — the product grades restart via `start.sh`, and rewriting supervision later is wasteful. Full Redis-outage **fan-out fallback** logic stays in a future realtime task; this task only respawns Redis.\n\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\n\nNeighborhood scan of the repo found only `timer.sh`, empty `data/`, and agent prompt/gitignore files — no existing HTTP/Redis/SPA code. From the product instruction / epic, the following are **intentionally excluded** from MARATHON-2:\n\n| Item | Why excluded |\n|---|---|\n| `GET /` SPA / static assets / `data-testid` hooks | Frontend tasks; not in this task description |\n| Auth register/login/me (MARATHON-3) | Separate task; needs DB schema |\n| Profiles, workspaces, channels, messages, files, search, etc. | Later epic children |\n| WebSocket `/api/ws` + dense `seq` + cross-node fan-out | Realtime task(s); needs Redis pub/sub usage + fallback |\n| IRC gateway `:6667` | Separate protocol task |\n| Redis-outage application-level fan-out fallback (direct peer push) | Requires message pipeline; only process respawn is in scope here |\n| Dependency on Slack SDK / remote APIs | Prohibited entirely |\n\nIf the neighborhood later gains half-built servers on these ports, implementers must replace/integrate with this layout rather than run a second cluster.\n\n## Edge Cases & Error Handling\n\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\n|---|---|\n| Port 8000/8001/8002 already bound at start | Fail fast with a clear stderr message; exit non-zero. Do not silently bind another port. (QA uses `marathon-freeports` / `marathon-portlock`.) |\n| Port 6379 already bound by an alien process | Fail fast if it is not our Redis; if a previous Huddle Redis is healthy, either adopt it or kill-and-replace — **prefer**: if `PING` works on `127.0.0.1:6379`, reuse; otherwise abort with error. |\n| `redis-server` binary missing | Exit non-zero with message; do not pretend health is enough. |\n| HTTP child SIGKILL’d | Supervisor restarts that node only; other nodes keep serving without interruption. |\n| Redis SIGKILL’d | Supervisor restarts Redis; HTTP nodes keep answering `/api/health` (and later writes once those exist). Do **not** crash nodes when Redis drops. |\n| `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` missing/invalid | Node process exits immediately with error; supervisor may restart — ensure `start.sh` always exports valid env so this is a developer mistake, not steady state. |\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGTERM/SIGINT) | Tear down children; do not leave orphaned redis/node processes. |\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGKILL) | Best-effort only; orphans possible — acceptable for grader stop. |\n| Duplicate start of `start.sh` | Second instance should fail on bind; do not require a pidfile unless implementer wants one under `/app/data`. |\n| Health called during Redis outage | Still `200 {\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":N}`. |\n| Extra JSON fields on health | Avoid; stick to exact two fields so graders using exact match do not flake. |\n| `status` value other than `\"ok\"` | Not used on success path. |\n| Pip/venv install failure | Exit non-zero before binding ports; log the pip error. |\n| Working directory | `start.sh` `cd`s to its own directory (`APP_ROOT`) so paths work regardless of caller cwd. |\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. `test -x /app/start.sh` (or `./start.sh` in repo root) succeeds.\n2. After starting `./start.sh` (under `marathon-portlock` when ports might be contested) and waiting for readiness:\n   - `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health` → HTTP 200, JSON `status==\"ok\"`, `node_id==0`\n   - same for `:8001` → `node_id==1`\n   - same for `:8002` → `node_id==2`\n3. `redis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING` → `PONG`.\n4. `start.sh` remains in the foreground (does not daemonize itself and return immediately).\n5. `node_id` values are JSON numbers; response contains no requirement for other keys.\n6. Killing one node’s PID (SIGKILL) does **not** take down the other two health endpoints; within 60s the killed port serves health again with the **same** `node_id`.\n7. Killing `redis-server` does **not** cause health endpoints to fail; Redis accepts `PING` again after `start.sh` respawns it.\n8. No Docker socket usage; no calls to external network services required for health.\n9. A clear module path exists for adding routes (e.g. empty aggregation point in `huddle/api/__init__.py`) so MARATHON-3 can attach `/api/auth/*` without changing `start.sh`.\n10. Negative: binding must **not** use `0.0.0.0` for the three HTTP nodes (must be `127.0.0.1`).\n11. Negative: `/api/health` must **not** return `5xx` solely because Redis is down.\n12. Negative: `node_id` for port 8000 must **not** be `1` or `2` under any restart ordering.\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n**Automated / implementer**\n- Unit: config mapping port↔`node_id`; reject mismatched env.\n- Unit: health handler returns exact payload for each injected `node_id`.\n- Integration (script or pytest): boot via `start.sh` (or invoke three modules + redis in a test harness), assert three health payloads + Redis PING.\n- Chaos smoke: SIGKILL one uvicorn PID and Redis PID; assert criteria 6–7.\n- Always free ports first: `marathon-freeports`; wrap long-lived servers with `marathon-portlock`. Kill all started processes before finishing.\n\n**Human / QA verification**\n```bash\nmarathon-freeports\nmarathon-portlock /app/start.sh   # or ./start.sh from repo root\n# in another shell:\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/api/health\nredis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING\n# SIGKILL one node PID; re-curl within 60s\n# SIGKILL redis; confirm health still 200; wait for PING\n```\n\n**Out of test scope for this task:** auth flows, SPA, IRC, WebSocket seq denseness, Redis fan-out fallback semantics beyond process respawn.\n","localExecutionTimeMs":75},"isBackground":false},"description":"Fetch MARATHON-2 design and notes"},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-797cdd47-f954-47c6-9f86-1c5f44fb39d5-8\nfc_93232823-f951-9f66-acd3-b4b9a27aed41_0","startedAtMs":"1787424883223","completedAtMs":"1787424883365"},"model_call_id":"a239700f-d856-4f40-b0ea-707f1bd6481c-3-7z8z","session_id":"08c39eea-4edb-41e1-83bc-3c69b9e09ad6","timestamp_ms":1787424883408}
