el_call_id":"a239700f-d856-4f40-b0ea-707f1bd6481c-3-7z8z","session_id":"08c39eea-4edb-41e1-83bc-3c69b9e09ad6","timestamp_ms":1787424884739}
{"type":"tool_call","subtype":"completed","call_id":"call-797cdd47-f954-47c6-9f86-1c5f44fb39d5-9\nfc_93232823-f951-9f66-acd3-b4b9a27aed41_1","tool_call":{"shellToolCall":{"args":{"command":"loom data show MARATHON-21 --output json | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(\"NOTES:\"); print(d.get(\"notes\") or \"(none)\"); print(\"\\n==== DESIGN ====\\n\"); print(d.get(\"design\") or \"(none)\")'","workingDirectory":"","timeout":30000,"toolCallId":"call-797cdd47-f954-47c6-9f86-1c5f44fb39d5-9\nfc_93232823-f951-9f66-acd3-b4b9a27aed41_1","simpleCommands":["loom","python3"],"hasInputRedirect":false,"hasOutputRedirect":false,"parsingResult":{"parsingFailed":false,"executableCommands":[{"name":"loom","args":[{"type":"word","value":"data"},{"type":"word","value":"show"},{"type":"word","value":"MARATHON-21"},{"type":"word","value":"--output"},{"type":"word","value":"json"}],"fullText":"loom data show MARATHON-21 --output json"},{"name":"python3","args":[{"type":"word","value":"-c"},{"type":"raw_string","value":"'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(\"NOTES:\"); print(d.get(\"notes\") or \"(none)\"); print(\"\\n==== DESIGN ====\\n\"); print(d.get(\"design\") or \"(none)\")'"}],"fullText":"python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(\"NOTES:\"); print(d.get(\"notes\") or \"(none)\"); print(\"\\n==== DESIGN ====\\n\"); print(d.get(\"design\") or \"(none)\")'"}],"hasRedirects":false,"hasCommandSubstitution":false,"redirects":[]},"fileOutputThresholdBytes":"40000","isBackground":false,"skipApproval":false,"timeoutBehavior":"TIMEOUT_BEHAVIOR_BACKGROUND","hardTimeout":86400000,"description":"Fetch MARATHON-21 design and notes","closeStdin":true,"conversationId":"08c39eea-4edb-41e1-83bc-3c69b9e09ad6","adminCommandDenylist":[]},"result":{"success":{"command":"loom data show MARATHON-21 --output json | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(\"NOTES:\"); print(d.get(\"notes\") or \"(none)\"); print(\"\\n==== DESIGN ====\\n\"); print(d.get(\"design\") or \"(none)\")'","workingDirectory":"","exitCode":0,"signal":"","stdout":"NOTES:\nDesign complete for HTTP node auto-restart SLA. Builds on MARATHON-2 supervisor; hardens start.sh only (reap, bind-retry, set -e safety, ≤60s health recovery, survivors untouched). No app/API schema changes. Route to backend implementer after lead removes `architect` (label `backend` already present).\n\n==== DESIGN ====\n\n## Summary\n\nGuarantee that `/app/start.sh` (repo-root `start.sh`) automatically respawns any HTTP node that exits for any reason—including SIGKILL—so that `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, and `:8002` each answer `GET /api/health` again within **60 seconds wall-clock**, with **no external action**. Surviving nodes must keep serving throughout. MARATHON-2 already shipped a foreground supervisor and a 1s poll loop; this task owns the **hard 60s SLA**, death-detection/reap correctness, bind-retry behavior, and acceptance criteria for HTTP-only chaos. Redis respawn remains as designed in MARATHON-2 and must not regress, but is not the graded focus here.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n**Decision:** Harden the existing bash supervisor in `start.sh`—do not introduce systemd, supervisord, Docker, a second process manager, or an in-Python watchdog. Detection stays PID-based (children started by `start.sh`); readiness for the SLA is HTTP-based (`GET /api/health` → 200 on that port).\n\n**Why**\n- The product and MARATHON-2 already lock three OS processes + bash ownership of lifecycle; rewriting supervision would break the epic’s ops seam.\n- A poll interval of ~1s (or event-driven `wait -n` plus a short sleep) easily meets a 60s budget if death is detected and respawn does not abort the supervisor.\n- Health readiness (not merely “PID exists”) matches how the grader and clients observe recovery.\n\n**Alternatives considered**\n1. **Leave MARATHON-2 loop unchanged** — notes claim ~2s recovery, but this P0 task exists as a dedicated graded slice; without explicit reap, bind-retry, and “supervisor must not exit on child death” rules, implementers can leave latent races. Rejected as “design = no-op.”\n2. **External process manager (systemd user unit, supervisord)** — violates self-contained `/app/start.sh` foreground contract. Rejected.\n3. **In-process multi-port single OS process** — fails SIGKILL-one-node model. Rejected (already rejected in MARATHON-2).\n4. **Health-only probing without PID tracking** — can false-positive during slow starts and does not reap zombies; usable only as a secondary signal. Rejected as primary detector.\n\n**Trade-off accepted:** Brief connection failures on the killed port during the outage window are expected; clients must retry (SPA/WebSocket reconnect owned by later tasks). The supervisor optimizes for simple, local PID+poll reliability rather than zero-downtime migrate.\n\n**Consistency with MARATHON-2 (authoritative sibling)**\n- Keep port↔`node_id` map: `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`.\n- Keep env: `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`, `HUDDLE_PORT`, `HUDDLE_HOST`, `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL`, `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR`.\n- Keep `GET /api/health` body `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":N}` unchanged.\n- MARATHON-2 text said “do not defer HTTP auto-restart to a later task”; the harness nonetheless created MARATHON-21. **This design does not diverge on mechanism**—it narrows ownership: MARATHON-21 is the SLA/hardening of the HTTP restart path already sketched in MARATHON-2, not a second supervisor.\n\n## Interface Contracts\n\n### Ops seam (verifier / QA ↔ `start.sh`) — **primary contract for this task**\n\n| Concern | Contract |\n|---|---|\n| Entrypoint | Executable `start.sh` at app root (`/app/start.sh` in runtime layout). |\n| Lifetime | Stays in the **foreground** until SIGINT/SIGTERM (or equivalent stop). |\n| HTTP children | Exactly three node processes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002` with stable `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT`. |\n| Auto-restart | If any HTTP child exits (SIGKILL, SIGTERM, crash, bind failure exit), `start.sh` respawns **that** node only, same id/port, without operator intervention. |\n| SLA | From the moment the process is gone, wall-clock time until `curl`/`GET http://127.0.0.1:<port>/api/health` returns **200** with correct `node_id` is **≤ 60s**. Target remains a few seconds. |\n| Survivors | While one port is down, the other two must continue to return 200 health (and any later APIs already mounted). |\n| Shutdown | SIGINT/SIGTERM to `start.sh` still tears down children (MARATHON-2); restart loop must not treat intentional shutdown as a respawn storm after trap runs. |\n\n**Ownership:** Backend / ops only. No frontend change in this task.\n\n### HTTP seam — `GET /api/health` (unchanged from MARATHON-2)\n\n| Side | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Server (each node) | Return `200` `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":<0\\|1\\|2>}` when the process is listening; do not probe Redis/SQLite. |\n| Client (grader, future SPA) | Treat TCP refuse / timeout / non-200 as “this node down”; **retry**. Do not require all three nodes for reads that can hit a live peer. |\n| While killed node is down | Clients using that port see connection errors; clients on surviving ports are unaffected. |\n| After respawn | Same port, same `node_id`; no session stickiness invented here. |\n\n**Validation:** Server validates `HUDDLE_*` at process start (existing `huddle/config.py`). Client validates HTTP status + JSON keys for health checks.\n\n**Slow / fail behavior:** No new API errors. Outage is a transport failure, not an application error body.\n\n### Frontend / realtime seam (informational; not implemented here)\n\n| Consumer | Expected behavior during single-node kill |\n|---|---|\n| Future SPA (MARATHON-24+) | If its API base URL points at the dead port, show retry/reconnect; do not assume cluster-wide outage. |\n| WebSocket (MARATHON-20) | Connection on dead node drops; resume/subscribe against a live node is that task’s problem. Restart only restores a peer process. |\n| Cross-node fan-out (MARATHON-22) | May see a peer gap until respawn; must not require this task to change pub/sub code. |\n\nPayload shapes crossing the FE/BE JSON seam are **unchanged** by this task.\n\n## Data Model\n\nNo schema changes. No new entities, files under `data/`, or Redis key formats.\n\n| Store | Role for this task |\n|---|---|\n| In-supervisor state | Associative map `node_id → child PID` (existing `NODE_PIDS`); optional generation/restart counters for logs only. |\n| SQLite / Redis data | Untouched; shared durable state must remain valid across respawn (already implied by shared `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR`). |\n\n**Backward compatible:** yes (behavior-only change to `start.sh`).\n\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\n\n### Modify\n\n| File | Change |\n|---|---|\n| `start.sh` | Harden HTTP supervisor path: (1) **reap** exited node PIDs with non-blocking/`wait \"$pid\"` before clearing/`start_node`; (2) detect down via `kill -0` failure **after** reap attempt (and treat missing PID as down); (3) respawn only the dead `node_id`/`port` via existing `start_node`; (4) ensure child exit or failed bind **never** causes `set -e` to tear down the whole cluster—restart loop must continue until 60s SLA is met on retry; (5) keep poll cadence ≤ ~2s (current `sleep 1` is acceptable); (6) on post-kill port still busy, skip/fail that spawn attempt, log, retry next iteration—do not `die`; (7) preserve Redis supervision and INT/TERM trap from MARATHON-2; (8) log restart events with `node_id`, port, old/new PID. |\n\n### Do not create (unless implementer finds a tiny helper unavoidable)\n\n| File | Reason |\n|---|---|\n| New Python modules | No app-level restart API. |\n| systemd / compose / Procfile | Forbidden pattern for this epic. |\n| Changes to `huddle/api/health.py`, `huddle/config.py`, `huddle/app.py` | Contracts already correct; only touch if a proven bug blocks restart identity (unexpected). |\n\n### Create\n\nNone required.\n\n## Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\n\n| Order | Dependency |\n|---|---|\n| Prerequisite | **MARATHON-2** (closed): cluster scaffold, health, basic supervisor — **done**. |\n| This task | Can implement immediately; no dependency on auth/SPA/WS. |\n| Parallel | Any API/SPA task may proceed; they must tolerate transient single-port refuse. |\n| Downstream | MARATHON-20/22/23 assume peers can disappear and return; they consume the ops seam this task hardens but do not own it. |\n\n**Seams this task owns**\n- Contract name: **HTTP node auto-restart SLA** (ops ↔ `start.sh`).\n- Consumers: QA/grader, MARATHON-20 (WS), MARATHON-22 (fan-out), MARATHON-23 (IRC may be separate process—out of scope here).\n\n**Seams this task consumes**\n- MARATHON-2: port map, `start_node` env contract, `GET /api/health`.\n\n**Split follow-ups?** No. Do not split Redis restart (already MARATHON-2) or hang detection (not required) into this ticket.\n\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\n\nNeighborhood scan (`start.sh`, `huddle/**`, `*.sh`, restart/supervisor greps):\n\n| Item | Why excluded |\n|---|---|\n| `timer.sh` | Harness countdown helper; unrelated to cluster supervision. |\n| Redis auto-restart path in `start.sh` | Owned/graded under MARATHON-2; do not remove, but do not expand Redis semantics here. |\n| `huddle/api/health.py` / config / app factory | Stable; no change required for restart. |\n| IRC process on `:6667` (MARATHON-23) | Not an HTTP node; separate supervisor rules when that task lands. |\n| WebSocket resume after peer death (MARATHON-20) | Client/protocol concern. |\n| Redis-outage fan-out fallback (MARATHON-22) | Application messaging path, not process respawn. |\n| SPA reconnect UX (MARATHON-24+) | Frontend. |\n| Detecting hung-but-alive nodes (event loop deadlock) | Task specifies SIGKILL/exit respawn, not liveness beyond process death. |\n| Load balancer / multi-host failover | Single-host three-port model only. |\n| `loom-prompts/` | Agent prompts; not product code. |\n\n## Edge Cases & Error Handling\n\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\n|---|---|\n| One HTTP child SIGKILL’d | Reap PID; restart that `node_id`/`port` only; other nodes uninterrupted; health on killed port back within ≤60s with same `node_id`. |\n| All three SIGKILL’d in sequence | Restart each as detected; all three healthy again within 60s of each respective kill (not “60s for the batch only” as an excuse to delay the first). |\n| Child exits immediately (bad env, import error) | Log error; retry on poll interval; **do not** exit `start.sh` after initial successful cluster start. (Fatal `die` remains allowed only during initial `ensure_deps` / first bind checks before the supervisor loop.) |\n| Port still in `TIME_WAIT` / briefly busy after kill | Log; do not `die`; retry next loop iteration until bind succeeds; still meet 60s if OS releases promptly (typical). |\n| Respawn gets a new PID | Update `NODE_PIDS[node_id]`; never reuse a stale PID for liveness. |\n| Zombie / unreaped child | Always `wait` the recorded PID when down or after detected exit so the process table cannot fool later checks. |\n| `kill -0` race (PID reused by unrelated process) | Prefer wait/reap bookkeeping; only start one child per `node_id`; optional sanity: if PID alive but port not listening for multiple polls, kill and restart **that** child only (implementer may add if needed to meet SLA; not required if PID tracking is correct). |\n| Supervisor receives SIGINT/SIGTERM | Trap runs `stop_children`; exit; **no** respawn after shutdown begins. |\n| Supervisor receives SIGKILL | Orphans possible; acceptable (MARATHON-2). |\n| Redis down during HTTP restart | HTTP restart still proceeds; health must not depend on Redis (MARATHON-2). |\n| Duplicate `start.sh` | Still fail fast on ports in use at initial start; out of scope to soft-adopt alien node PIDs. |\n| Logging | Restarts go to stderr via existing `[huddle]` logger; no new log file required. |\n| `set -euo pipefail` | Conditionals around `kill`/`wait`/`port` checks must be written so a down child cannot abort the loop. |\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. With cluster up via `./start.sh`, SIGKILL the PID of **one** HTTP node (the process serving one of 8000/8001/8002).\n2. Continuously, the **other two** ports return `GET /api/health` → 200 with their correct `node_id` throughout the outage (sample at least once during the down window).\n3. Within **60 seconds** of the kill, the killed port returns `GET /api/health` → 200 with the **same** `node_id` as before (8000→0, 8001→1, 8002→2).\n4. No manual restart, no second terminal intervention, no re-running `start.sh`—the original foreground supervisor performs the respawn.\n5. Repeat criteria 1–4 for each of the three ports (can be sequential).\n6. Negative: killing one node must **not** cause `start.sh` to exit.\n7. Negative: killing one node must **not** restart or kill the other two node PIDs as a side effect.\n8. Negative: after respawn, `node_id` on port 8000 must **not** become 1 or 2 (identity stays port-derived).\n9. Negative: recovery must **not** require Docker, remote services, or editing config mid-test.\n10. `start.sh` remains foreground for the whole test; Redis PING still works unless Redis was also killed (Redis kill+respawn may be smoke-checked but is not the primary AC of this task).\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n**Implementer**\n- Chaos script: boot under `marathon-portlock` / free ports; record PIDs (e.g. from `ss`/`lsof` on each port or supervisor logs); `kill -9` one PID; poll health on all three ports every ~1s; assert survivors always 200; assert victim 200 by t≤60 with correct `node_id`.\n- Repeat for each port; optionally double-kill two nodes and confirm both recover without killing the third intentionally.\n- Regression: SIGINT to `start.sh` still shuts down children (no infinite respawn after trap).\n- Confirm supervisor loop does not exit when a child returns non-zero.\n\n**Human / QA**\n```bash\nmarathon-freeports\nmarathon-portlock ./start.sh\n# other shell:\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/api/health\n# identify PID listening on 8001; kill -9 $PID\n# while true; do date; curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\n' http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health; \\\n#   curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health; sleep 1; done\n# expect 8000/8002 healthy throughout; 8001 healthy again within 60s with node_id=1\n```\n\n**Out of test scope:** SPA behavior, WS resume, IRC, auth, Redis fan-out fallback logic beyond “HTTP nodes stay up / return.”\n","stderr":"time=2026-08-22T18:54:44.794Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\n","executionTime":121,"interleavedOutput":"time=2026-08-22T18:54:44.794Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\nNOTES:\nDesign complete for HTTP node auto-restart SLA. Builds on MARATHON-2 supervisor; hardens start.sh only (reap, bind-retry, set -e safety, ≤60s health recovery, survivors untouched). No app/API schema changes. Route to backend implementer after lead removes `architect` (label `backend` already present).\n\n==== DESIGN ====\n\n## Summary\n\nGuarantee that `/app/start.sh` (repo-root `start.sh`) automatically respawns any HTTP node that exits for any reason—including SIGKILL—so that `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, and `:8002` each answer `GET /api/health` again within **60 seconds wall-clock**, with **no external action**. Surviving nodes must keep serving throughout. MARATHON-2 already shipped a foreground supervisor and a 1s poll loop; this task owns the **hard 60s SLA**, death-detection/reap correctness, bind-retry behavior, and acceptance criteria for HTTP-only chaos. Redis respawn remains as designed in MARATHON-2 and must not regress, but is not the graded focus here.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n**Decision:** Harden the existing bash supervisor in `start.sh`—do not introduce systemd, supervisord, Docker, a second process manager, or an in-Python